// Every message satl can say about a program, as data. DESIGN §9 is the
// specification and this file is the half a compiler can check.
//
// THERE IS NO GENERATOR AND NO BUILD STEP. This is an X-macro list, the same
// mechanism words.def uses and FORMAT/CXX.md §7 describes: a consumer defines
// SAT_CODE, includes this file, and undefines it again. The enum, the sentence
// table, the severity table, the arity table, the invariants and
// `satl --errors` therefore all come from ONE list and cannot drift apart.
//
// WHAT THE FIRST SATELLITE DID INSTEAD IS THE REASON THIS FILE EXISTS. DESIGN
// §9 counts it: 199 `fail()` call sites composing 224 distinct string literals
// between them, with no codes, no stack and no suggestions. A message that
// lives at its call site cannot be looked up, cannot be counted, and cannot be
// found again when somebody wants to know whether two sites say the same thing
// in two ways. Every row below is one sentence in one place.

// --- how a row is read ------------------------------------------------------
//
//     SAT_CODE(number, ident, severity, text)
//
// THE NUMBER IS A COLUMN HERE AND IT IS A POSITION IN words.def, and the
// difference is deliberate rather than an inconsistency between two registries.
// A word's number is what a program MEANS -- DESIGN §4.3 freezes registration
// order because inserting a row silently renumbers every sibling and every
// already-written program with it. An error code is what a PERSON LOOKS UP: it
// has to survive a row being deleted, has to leave gaps where a milestone
// reserved a block, and has to be assignable out of order. Those are opposite
// requirements and they get opposite encodings. What replaces "a number is a
// position" as the safety net is words_invariants.hpp's trick pointed at this
// table instead: the numbers must ASCEND, which catches an insertion, and the
// switch in codes.hpp catches a duplicate as `error: duplicate case value`.
//
// `severity` is one of two things:
//
//     SAT_ERROR   something is wrong and satl did not do what was asked
//     SAT_NOTE    a second place worth looking at, or a fact about the first
//
// A NOTE IS A ROW HERE AND NOT A STRING AT A CALL SITE, which is the whole
// argument of this file applied to the half that is easiest to exempt from it.
// DESIGN §9 asks for "notes carrying their own spans"; a note whose sentence
// lived beside the error that raises it would be the 224 literals again, at
// half the volume and with none of the visibility.
//
// `text` is the sentence, with `{1}`, `{2}`, `{3}` where the site fills a hole.
// THE ARITY IS NOT A COLUMN -- it is the highest hole in the text, counted at
// compile time, and codes.hpp asserts the holes run 1..N with none missing. A
// column would be a second place the same fact lives, and the one that goes
// stale is always the column.
//
// AND THE ARITY IS CHECKED AT THE CALL SITE, which is the property this whole
// arrangement is for: errors::make<Code::X>(span, a, b) is a template, so a
// site that passes two arguments to a three-hole sentence is a compile error
// naming the code. The first satellite could not have had that check, because
// the sentence was the argument.

// --- the blocks -------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The first two digits say WHO IS COMPLAINING, which is what makes a code worth
// four digits rather than two. A reader who has S0231 in front of them knows it
// came from the parser before they look it up, and a milestone that adds codes
// takes a block instead of taking the next free number and interleaving itself
// with everybody else.
//
//     S01xx   the lexer                     DESIGN §5      M3
//     S02xx   the parser                    DESIGN §6      M4
//     S03xx   the `.satc` cache             SATC.md        M4.5
//     S04xx   the file satl was given                      M5/M10
//     S05xx   resolve -- names and slots    DESIGN §7      M7
//     S06xx   numbers                       DESIGN §8.1    M8
//     S07xx   the evaluator                                M9/M10
//     S08xx   the machine limits            PLAN §4.5      M6
//     S09xx   the clock and the dice        DESIGN §11/§13 M13
//     S10xx   the console's input           DESIGN §10.1   M14
//     S11xx   satellite.help                DESIGN §4.6    M18
//
// The reserved blocks are written down and NOT populated. A block reserved in a
// comment costs nothing and stops the thing this file exists to stop, which is
// two milestones reaching for the same number on the same day.
//
// M6 TOOK S08xx AND NOT S04xx, AND THE PARAGRAPH THAT STOOD HERE ASKED FOR THAT
// CHECK BY NAME. It said "S08xx being the free one is the answer only until
// somebody checks whether the machine limits belong beside S04xx -- the file
// satl was given -- instead." Checked, 2026-08-30, and they do not. S04xx's own
// block note says what it is: "the command line was well formed and named a
// file, and the file is where it stopped" -- a file the USER GAVE satl, holding
// a program the user wrote. A satellite_config.ini is a file satl WENT LOOKING
// FOR, beside its own binary, holding settings an installer wrote; the ordinary
// run never names it and never sees it. The first two digits say who is
// complaining, and the complainer here is the limits reader rather than the arm
// that opens what it was handed.
//
// IT IS OUT OF ORDER WITH THE RESERVATIONS ABOVE, DELIBERATELY. S05, S06 and S07
// are M7's, M8's and M9's, and M6 lands before all three -- so the block numbers
// no longer run in the order the milestones do. **S05xx was taken on 2026-08-31
// and nothing moved to make room for it**, which is the whole of what a
// reservation buys and is the first time this file has been able to say it
// happened rather than that it would. That is what a reserved block IS
// for: this file's whole argument is that a block is taken and written down so
// that "two milestones reaching for the same number on the same day" cannot
// happen, and honouring three reservations by taking the fourth number is that
// working, not a defect in it. A code is a column and not a position (see the
// paragraph on how a row is read), so nothing about the numbering says the
// blocks are in build order, and nothing should be inferred from it.

#ifndef SAT_CODE
#define SAT_CODE(number, ident, severity, text)
#endif

// The two severities, as values, so the numbers live here and only there --
// read back as errors::kError and errors::kNote. NOT #undef'd at the bottom
// with the macro above, for that reason.
#ifndef SAT_ERROR
#define SAT_ERROR 1
#endif
#ifndef SAT_NOTE
#define SAT_NOTE 0
#endif

// --- S01xx -- the lexer -----------------------------------------------------
//
// ONE ERROR, AND THAT IS THE LEXER'S DESIGN RATHER THAN AN OMISSION. DESIGN
// §5.6 says a byte the code table has no entry for becomes a Punct holding that
// byte rather than an error, "because the lexer's job is to describe the file,
// and deciding that a character is meaningless is the parser's". So the only
// thing that can stop a lex is a string literal with no end.

SAT_CODE(101, LEX_UNTERMINATED_STRING, SAT_ERROR,
         "this `\"` opens a string literal and nothing closes it before the "
         "end of the line -- put a `\"` where the text is meant to stop")

SAT_CODE(102, LEX_LINE_ENDS_HERE, SAT_NOTE,
         "the line ends here, and a string literal cannot carry on to the "
         "next one")

// --- S02xx -- the parser ----------------------------------------------------
//
// 200-209 is the cursor and the four loops, 210-219 statements, 220-229 types,
// 230-239 expressions, 240-249 names. The gaps are on purpose; see the block
// note above.

SAT_CODE(201, PARSE_EXPECTED_PUNCT, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected `{1}` {2}, and found {3} instead")

SAT_CODE(202, PARSE_EXPECTED_WORD, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected {1}, and found {2} instead")

SAT_CODE(203, PARSE_STATEMENT_ALREADY_ENDED, SAT_ERROR,
         "a statement ends at the end of its line, and {1} is a second one on "
         "the same line -- put it on a line of its own")

SAT_CODE(204, PARSE_EXPECTED_DECLARATION, SAT_ERROR,
         "a file holds satellite.include, satellite.capsule, "
         "satellite.spacesuit and satellite.library.<name>, and {1} is none of "
         "them")

SAT_CODE(205, PARSE_EXPECTED_STATEMENT, SAT_ERROR,
         "a block holds statements, and {1} does not begin one")

SAT_CODE(206, PARSE_EXPECTED_SUIT_ITEM, SAT_ERROR,
         "a spacesuit holds fields, capsules, and satellite.protected or "
         "satellite.public sections, and {1} is none of them")

SAT_CODE(207, PARSE_EXPECTED_FIELD_OR_CAPSULE, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected a field or a satellite.capsule, and found {1}")

// SAID RATHER THAN SHOWN BY THE OUTPUT SIMPLY STOPPING. parser.cpp reports at
// most 20 errors and then leaves the rest of the file unread; without this the
// twenty-first problem is invisible and the file looks understood.
SAT_CODE(208, PARSE_TOO_MANY_ERRORS, SAT_NOTE,
         "{1} problems were reported and the rest of the file was not read -- "
         "fix these and run it again")

SAT_CODE(210, PARSE_STATEMENT_NEEDS_A_WORD, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.statement is written with one of if, else, while or for "
         "after it")

// NAMED RATHER THAN LUMPED IN WITH "no such statement", because a stray `else`
// is a real thing a person writes and the useful sentence is about the `if`
// and not about the word.
SAT_CODE(211, PARSE_ELSE_WITHOUT_IF, SAT_ERROR,
         "this satellite.statement.else has no satellite.statement.if in front "
         "of it to belong to")

SAT_CODE(212, PARSE_NO_SUCH_STATEMENT, SAT_ERROR,
         "no statement is spelled `{1}` -- satellite.statement has if, else, "
         "while and for")

SAT_CODE(213, PARSE_DECLARATION_IN_BLOCK, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.{1} is a declaration and goes at the top of a file, not "
         "inside a block")

SAT_CODE(214, PARSE_ELSE_NEEDS_A_BLOCK, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.statement.else is followed by a block or by another "
         "satellite.statement.if, and found {1}")

SAT_CODE(215, PARSE_ASSIGN_TARGET, SAT_ERROR,
         "the left of an assignment has to name somewhere to write, and {1} "
         "does not")

SAT_CODE(220, PARSE_NOT_A_TYPE, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.{1} is not a type -- a type path is "
         "satellite.variable.<name> or satellite.container.<name>")

SAT_CODE(221, PARSE_EXPECTED_TYPE, SAT_ERROR,
         "a type is satellite.variable.<name>, satellite.container.<name>, "
         "satellite itself, or the name of a spacesuit -- and {1} is none of "
         "them")

// `>=` IS ONE TOKEN AND DESIGN §5.5 IS WHY IT STAYS ONE. The lexer matches it
// greedily and lexer.hpp's split_punct is deliberately never called, so the fix
// belongs in the program: a space.
SAT_CODE(222, PARSE_GENERIC_CLOSE_GE, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected `>` to close the type's arguments and found `>=`, which is "
         "one token -- write a space between the two characters")

SAT_CODE(230, PARSE_EXPECTED_SUBSCRIPT, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected an index or a slice inside the brackets, and found {1}")

SAT_CODE(231, PARSE_EXPECTED_EXPRESSION, SAT_ERROR,
         "expected an expression -- a number, a string, a name, a path, or one "
         "in brackets -- and found {1}")

SAT_CODE(240, PARSE_NOT_A_LANGUAGE_CAPSULE, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.{1} is not a capsule the language owns -- the one reserved "
         "capsule name is satellite.main")

SAT_CODE(241, PARSE_NAME_IS_LANGUAGE_OWNED, SAT_ERROR,
         "the language already owns the name {1} under {2}, so it cannot be a "
         "{3}'s name")

SAT_CODE(242, PARSE_NAME_ALREADY_DEFINED, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is already the name of something in this program, and a name is "
         "declared once")

SAT_CODE(243, PARSE_NAME_UNNUMBERABLE, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} could not be given a number, which is satl's own limit and not "
         "anything wrong with this program")

// The parser's notes. 290-299, at the end of the block rather than beside the
// errors that raise them, because a note is reused: 290 is attached by nine
// call sites and belongs to none of them.
SAT_CODE(290, NOTE_OPENED_HERE, SAT_NOTE,
         "this `{1}` is the one that is not closed")

SAT_CODE(291, NOTE_FIRST_DECLARED_HERE, SAT_NOTE,
         "{1} was declared here")

// --- S03xx -- the `.satc` cache ---------------------------------------------
//
// SATC.md §4's four ways a cache file can be wrong, plus the sentence all four
// end with. M4.5 wrote these as string literals and said in its own header that
// "codes are M5's to design with all of them in view"; this is that.
//
// NONE OF THEM CARRIES A SPAN, and the reason is worth stating because the
// machinery is right here and unused. A `.satc` is not a file the user wrote --
// read.cpp's own argument -- so what they can act on is the file as a whole,
// which is exactly what the location line already names. A caret pointing at
// byte 412 of a generated file tells somebody to read a file that the next
// sentence tells them to delete.

SAT_CODE(301, SATC_NOT_A_SATC, SAT_ERROR,
         "this does not begin with a `satc` line, so it is not a `.satc` at all")

SAT_CODE(302, SATC_WRONG_VERSION, SAT_ERROR,
         "this is version {1} of the `.satc` format and this satl reads "
         "version {2}, so it was written by a different satl")

SAT_CODE(303, SATC_NO_BLANK_LINE, SAT_ERROR,
         "this has no blank line after its three header lines, so where the "
         "header ends cannot be told")

SAT_CODE(304, SATC_DID_NOT_PARSE, SAT_ERROR,
         "this did not parse: {1}")

SAT_CODE(305, SATC_MARK_WITHOUT_A_NUMBER, SAT_ERROR,
         "this has a `{1}` with no number after it, which is not something the "
         "writer can produce")

SAT_CODE(306, SATC_UNKNOWN_PATH, SAT_ERROR,
         "this names `{1}`, which this satl's numbering does not have")

// M19.6. `0#down` is only meaningful directly after the `(` of the call whose
// selector it is an option to -- satellite_cache/unnumber.cpp walks back over
// the word to find where the Mark belongs -- so an option token anywhere else
// is a file this writer did not produce. Refused rather than guessed at: the
// whole point of the token is to record a decision, and a decision attached to
// no call is worse than none.
SAT_CODE(307, SATC_OPTION_WITHOUT_A_CALL, SAT_ERROR,
         "this has the option `{1}` where no call's selector comes before it, "
         "which is not something the writer can produce")

SAT_CODE(390, NOTE_SATC_IGNORED, SAT_NOTE,
         "it was ignored and the source was read instead, so nothing is wrong "
         "with your program -- deleting the file is safe, because a `.satc` is "
         "only a cache")

// --- S04xx -- the file satl was given ---------------------------------------
//
// NOT THE COMMAND LINE, which is a different interface and keeps its own words
// in programs/opening.cpp. A code and a span are for something INSIDE a file;
// `satl --frobnicate` has no file and no place in one, and giving it a code
// would be a lookup number for a sentence that already names its own fix.
// This block is the seam between the two: the command line was well formed and
// named a file, and the file is where it stopped.

SAT_CODE(401, FILE_UNREADABLE, SAT_ERROR,
         "this file could not be read -- check the path, and that this user is "
         "allowed to open it")

// M10's ROW, AND IT IS IN THIS BLOCK RATHER THAN IN S07xx FOR THE REASON THE
// BLOCK NOTE ABOVE GIVES. The evaluator is not complaining: a file with no
// `satellite.main` parses, resolves and compiles perfectly, and every capsule
// in it can be run by `satl --call`. What has gone wrong is one level out --
// satl was handed a file to RUN and the file is not a program -- which is
// exactly "the command line was well formed and named a file, and the file is
// where it stopped".
//
// IT CARRIES NO SPAN, WHICH IS THE SECOND ROW IN THIS TREE TO DO SO AND THE
// FIRST WHERE IT IS THE INTERESTING PART. S0401 has none because the file never
// opened; this one has none because what is wrong with the file is something
// that is NOT IN IT, and there is no honest character to put a caret under.
// Pointing at line 1, or at the last capsule, would be the reporter inventing a
// place -- and DESIGN §9's carets are worth what they are because they have
// never done that.
SAT_CODE(402, FILE_NO_MAIN, SAT_ERROR,
         "this file declares no `satellite.main`, which is where a program "
         "starts -- `satl --call` runs one capsule by name instead")

// --- S05xx -- resolve: names and slots ---------------------------------------
//
// DESIGN §7 is the specification and PLAN M7 is the milestone. RESERVED SINCE
// M5 AND POPULATED HERE, which is the reservation mechanism working end to end:
// M6 landed in between and took S08xx rather than the next free number, so
// nothing had to move for this block to be taken.
//
// 500-509 the pass itself, 510-519 names, 520-529 paths and types, 530-539
// DESIGN §7.7's arguments object. The gaps are on purpose; see the block note
// at the top of this file.
//
// EVERY ONE OF THESE CARRIES A REAL SPAN, like S02xx and unlike S03xx. Resolve
// runs over the tree the user's own file produced, so every complaint has a
// token behind it and the caret lands under the word that is wrong.
//
// WHAT IS NOT HERE IS A TYPE ERROR, and the absence is a decision. This pass
// asks whether a name has somewhere to live and whether a path is a path; what
// a value IS -- DESIGN §8's model -- arrives at M9, and a milestone that
// checked `satellite.variable.number x = "a string"` here would be answering a
// question with a value model it does not have.

// 501 IS A NUMBER THAT MEANT SOMETHING ONCE AND IS NOT REUSED. RESOLVE_TOO_DEEP
// lived here for one day: M7 bounded the resolver at a fixed 2000 levels, and
// DESIGN §7.5 -- rewritten 2026-08-31 -- is that the language has no depth limit
// at all. This block's own note says a code "has to survive a row being deleted,
// has to leave gaps where a milestone reserved a block, and has to be assignable
// out of order". This is the first row this file has ever deleted, and the gap it
// leaves is that property being used rather than described.

SAT_CODE(511, RESOLVE_NO_SUCH_NAME, SAT_ERROR,
         "nothing called `{1}` is in scope here -- it is not a parameter, not "
         "a local declared above this line, and not a capsule this file "
         "declares")

SAT_CODE(512, RESOLVE_PARAMETER_TWICE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` names two parameters of this capsule, and a call gives one "
         "value to each")

// A NAME AND NOT A PATH, which is what makes this the resolver's rather than
// the parser's. `satellite` alone is a legal expression -- DESIGN §6's
// `primary` has it, and `satellite.return(satellite)` is the whole reason --
// so the parser accepts the word in every position a name may take and this is
// where declaring one is refused.
SAT_CODE(513, RESOLVE_NAME_IS_RESERVED, SAT_ERROR,
         "`satellite` is the language's own name and every word in it hangs "
         "under that one -- so it cannot also be the name of a {1}")

// THE ONE DUPLICATE THE PARSER CANNOT SEE, and it is worth a row of its own for
// that reason. `define_name()` reports S0242 for a second capsule of the user's
// own, because the numbering refuses to allocate the name twice -- but
// `satellite.main` is LOOKED UP rather than defined, so a file with two of them
// parses clean and has two entry points. Resolve's first pass is the only place
// that can notice.
SAT_CODE(514, RESOLVE_CAPSULE_TWICE, SAT_ERROR,
         "this file declares {1} twice, and a call has to name one of them")

// THE ERROR satellite_cache/paths.cpp NAMES AND DECLINES TO RAISE. Its own
// comment says a path the numbering cannot account for is "M7's to refuse with
// M5's did-you-mean over the node the segment failed under", and the cache's
// job in the meantime is to hand the tree over unchanged. This is that refusal.
SAT_CODE(521, RESOLVE_NO_SUCH_WORD, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is not a word the language has under {2}")

SAT_CODE(522, RESOLVE_NO_SUCH_TYPE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is not a type the language has under {2}")

// SEPARATE FROM S0521 BECAUSE THE FIX IS DIFFERENT, which is the argument
// S0809 already makes one block down. A misspelled segment is answered by
// spelling it right; a shape mismatch is answered by counting the arguments,
// and the sentence has to say which of the two happened or the reader checks
// the wrong thing first.
SAT_CODE(523, RESOLVE_NO_SUCH_SHAPE, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is not written with {2} -- the language has it in {3}")

// WORD_NUMBERS §1.5's FOLD, REFUSED RATHER THAN LEFT TO THE RUNTIME. The legal
// options are not a table in this file: they are the sibling rows spelled
// `<word>_<option>`, so this sentence is filled from the numbering and a new
// option added to words.def is offered here with no edit at all.
SAT_CODE(524, RESOLVE_NO_SUCH_OPTION, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is not an option {2} has -- it has {3}")

// DESIGN §7.7's OPEN QUESTION, ANSWERED. "Declaring a parameter named X gets a
// plain list with no properties, silently. Under §9 that silence is wrong --
// the language should say so." It is said here, and only where somebody has
// plainly meant the object: the suggester has to answer with one of the
// spellings before this is raised, so a parameter that was never trying to be
// `arguments` is left alone and is an ordinary list.
//
// `argv` WAS THIS CODE'S WORKED EXAMPLE UNTIL 2026-09-09 AND IS NOW A SPELLING.
// The author added it to words.def as §7.7 said only the author could, so a
// parameter named `argv` gets the object rather than this error. THAT DID NOT
// MAKE THIS CODE WRONG AND IT WAS NOT DELETED: `argu`, `arrgs` and `argvs` are
// still near misses and still land here. The sentence below names the accepted
// spellings from the registry rather than from a list in this file, so the
// alias row is the whole of the edit -- which is the property that made the
// reversal one line instead of a sweep.
SAT_CODE(531, RESOLVE_ALMOST_ARGUMENTS, SAT_ERROR,
         "satellite.main's parameter is the arguments object when it is named "
         "{1}, and `{2}` is none of them -- it would be an ordinary list with "
         "none of the machine's answers on it")

SAT_CODE(532, RESOLVE_NO_SUCH_ARGUMENT_FIELD, SAT_ERROR,
         "the arguments object has no `{1}` on it -- DESIGN §7.7 lists what it "
         "holds, and {2} is what this build has")

// Resolve's notes. 590-599, at the end of the block rather than beside the
// errors that raise them -- the arrangement S02xx and S08xx already have, and
// for the reason errors.def's header gives: a note is reused and belongs to
// none of the sites that attach it.
SAT_CODE(590, NOTE_DECLARED_FIRST_HERE, SAT_NOTE,
         "`{1}` is the one that came first")

// --- S06xx -- numbers --------------------------------------------------------
//
// DESIGN §8.1 is the specification and PLAN M8 is the milestone. RESERVED SINCE
// M5 AND POPULATED HERE, which is the second time this file has been able to say
// a reservation was honoured end to end: M6 took S08xx rather than the next free
// number, M7 took S05xx and nothing moved, and this block was waiting where it
// was written down.
//
// 600-609 the arithmetic itself, 610-619 what a number can be written as. The
// gaps are on purpose; see the block note at the top of this file.
//
// THREE ROWS AND NOT THIRTY, AND THE SMALLNESS IS THE MILESTONE. Nothing
// EXECUTES until M10, so the only site that can raise one of these today is
// `satl --number`, which is M8's consumer. The evaluator's own arithmetic
// errors are S07xx and are M9's -- a row added here for a message no site can
// send would be this file's own argument turned inside out.
//
// AND THERE IS NO OVERFLOW ROW, WHICH IS DESIGN §7.5 RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.
// The language has no limits: a number's size is bounded by memory the way a
// list's length is, so there is no width at which addition refuses. What ends a
// runaway is M6's watchdog at MEMORY_MAX, in words and with exit 4, and that is
// the same sentence a walker filling the heap gets.

SAT_CODE(601, NUMBER_DIVIDE_BY_ZERO, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` cannot be divided by zero -- every other number has an answer "
         "and this one has none, so satellite will not invent one")

// 602 AND 603 ARRIVED AT M15 WITH THE OPERATIONS THAT CAN RAISE THEM -- sqrt
// and power, the class-3 rows DESIGN §8.6 held behind the rounding rule --
// which is this block's own note working as written: "a row added here for a
// message no site can send would be this file's argument turned inside out",
// and now the sites exist. NEITHER PRINTS THE VALUE, which is S0711's rule
// rather than S0601's: a number in a sentence can be fifty thousand digits,
// and what a person needs is the fact, not the digits.

SAT_CODE(602, NUMBER_NO_REAL_ROOT, SAT_ERROR,
         "a square root was asked of a negative number, and no real number "
         "squares to a negative -- satellite has no imaginary type and will "
         "not invent one")

SAT_CODE(603, NUMBER_NEGATIVE_FRACTIONAL_POWER, SAT_ERROR,
         "a negative number was raised to a fractional power -- a real "
         "answer exists only for some written exponents ((-32)^0.2 is -2, "
         "(-32)^0.5 is not real), and an answer that comes and goes with the "
         "spelling of the exponent is not one satellite will stand behind")

SAT_CODE(610, NUMBER_NOT_A_NUMBER, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is not a number -- digits, optionally a `.` with more digits, "
         "and optionally an `e` and an exponent")

SAT_CODE(611, NUMBER_UNKNOWN_OPERATOR, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is not an operator satellite has -- the five are `+`, `-`, `*`, "
         "`/` and `%`")

// 612 IS SPENT AND WILL NOT BE REUSED. It was S0612 NUMBER_OPERATOR_NOT_YET --
// "`%` is not built yet ... PLAN.md §8 puts it at M15 with the rounding rule it
// needs" -- and it was written on 2026-08-31 for a claim that turned out to be
// wrong the same day. DESIGN §8.6 specifies modulus completely and puts it in
// the class it calls "exact and bounded, never rounds", so `%` was never
// waiting on M15's rounding rule; `satl --number 7 % 2` answers 1 and the row
// has no site left to be raised from. Deleted and not repurposed: this file's
// header says an error code "is what a PERSON LOOKS UP" and "has to survive a
// row being deleted", and a number that meant two things at two dates is the
// one thing that property does not survive.
//
// So the gap is the mechanism working, and it is the SECOND one this file has
// left by retiring a row rather than by reserving a block -- S0501 above is the
// first, deleted the same day and for the neighbouring reason. That one went
// because DESIGN §7.5 says the language has no depth limit; this one goes
// because DESIGN §8.6 had already specified the operation the row apologised
// for. Two gaps in one day is worth noticing: a registry that never loses a row
// is one whose rows were never checked against the documents they cite.

// --- S07xx -- the evaluator --------------------------------------------------
//
// PLAN M9 is the milestone and this is the block reserved for it since M5. THE
// THIRD RESERVATION HONOURED END TO END, and tests/reporter_test/codes.cpp has
// been counting: S05xx was claimed by M7 on 2026-08-31, S06xx by M8 the same
// day, and this is the last one that was being held. Nothing moved to make room
// for any of the three, which is the whole of what a reservation buys.
//
// 700-709 the control stack, 710-719 what a value can be used as, 720-729 what
// a program named that does not run yet, 730-739 the run stopped from OUTSIDE
// the program -- M11's decade, and the one whose rows are about nobody's
// mistake. The gaps are on purpose; see the block note at the top of this
// file.
//
// THE "NOT BUILT YET" ROW IS THE UNUSUAL ONE AND IT IS DELIBERATE. Every other
// code in this file is about a program that is wrong. S0720 is about a program
// that is RIGHT and arrived early: DESIGN §6's grammar parses a list literal, a
// spacesuit and a `satellite.include` today, and M16, M26 and M25 are what make
// them run. PLAN §8's opening rule is that a milestone is "a thing that works
// and can be demonstrated", which means every milestone between now and M28
// ships a language whose grammar is wider than its evaluator. Saying so with a
// code, a caret and the milestone's number is DESIGN §1.1 -- never do anything
// behind the user's back -- applied to the gap itself. The first satellite's
// answer to the same situation was a segfault or a silently wrong value.
//
// AND THERE IS NO ROW FOR ARITHMETIC OVERFLOW OR FOR A NUMBER GETTING TOO BIG,
// which is DESIGN §7.5 for the second time in this file. S06xx's block note
// already says it about `satellite_number`; it is true here for the same reason
// and about the same thing. What ends a runaway is memory, and S0701 is the one
// row in this block that says so -- it is a ceiling on a stack the user set,
// not a depth the language chose.

SAT_CODE(701, EVAL_TOO_DEEP, SAT_ERROR,
         "this recursion has filled the control stack -- {1} calls deep, "
         "holding {2}, and `satellite.library.system.max_depth` is {3}")

SAT_CODE(710, EVAL_NOT_A_CONDITION, SAT_ERROR,
         "a condition is a `satellite.variable.bool` and this one is {1} -- "
         "satellite has no truthiness, so a number cannot stand in for a test")

SAT_CODE(711, EVAL_NOT_A_NUMBER, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` works on two numbers and this one is {2}")

SAT_CODE(712, EVAL_NOT_COMPARABLE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` orders numbers, and {2} has no order -- `==` and `!=` work on "
         "anything, and nothing else does")

// THE NEXT FOUR ARE M11's, THE METHODS' ROWS, and 714 is the one DESIGN asked
// for by name: §6.4 qualification 3 wants "an undeclared variable" and "a
// declared variable holding nothing" to read differently, resolve owns the
// first at compile time, and this block owns the second at run time.
//
// 715 AND 716 REFUSE WHERE ANOTHER LANGUAGE WOULD HAND BACK A SENTINEL, and
// that is a decision, taken at M11 and overrulable in one row each:
// `"abc".at(9)` could answer something and `"abc".find("z")` could answer -1,
// and both would be a value quietly meaning "not a value" -- the thing DESIGN
// §1.1 exists to refuse. A refusal can LOOSEN into an answer later without
// changing any working program's meaning (M12 made "nothing" a thing a
// program can ask about, DESIGN §8.7); a sentinel, once a program is written
// against it, is forever.

SAT_CODE(713, EVAL_WRONG_TYPE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` wants {2} and this one is {3}")

SAT_CODE(714, EVAL_HOLDING_NOTHING, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` was asked of a variable that holds nothing -- a declared "
         "variable holds nothing until a value is assigned to it")

SAT_CODE(715, EVAL_PAST_THE_END, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` was asked about position {2} and this string has {3} "
         "characters -- the first is at 0, the last at one less than the "
         "count")

SAT_CODE(716, EVAL_NOT_FOUND, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` does not appear in this string, so `find` has no position to "
         "answer -- `contains(x)` is the question that answers false instead "
         "of stopping")

SAT_CODE(717, EVAL_NOWHERE_TO_WRITE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` changes its receiver, and this receiver names no place the "
         "change could be written back to -- DESIGN.md §6.4: a mutating "
         "method needs a receiver that names a storage slot")

SAT_CODE(718, EVAL_NEEDS_RECEIVER, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is a method and is asked on a value -- `my_variable.{1}` -- "
         "not written out as a path; DESIGN.md §6.4 keeps the long spelling "
         "off the surface on purpose")

SAT_CODE(719, EVAL_BACKWARDS, SAT_ERROR,
         "`substring` was asked from {1} to {2}, and a substring runs "
         "forwards -- the start is never past the end")

SAT_CODE(720, EVAL_NOT_BUILT, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} parses and does not run yet -- {2}")

SAT_CODE(721, EVAL_NO_HANDLER, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is a path satellite has a number for and nothing behind yet -- "
         "{2}")

SAT_CODE(722, EVAL_ARGUMENT_COUNT, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` takes {2} and was given {3}")

// 723 REPLACES A SENTENCE THAT GAVE WRONG ADVICE, WHICH IS WORSE THAN NO
// SENTENCE. Until M12, a method a declared type does not have -- `box.upper()`
// on a `variant`, `s.holding()` on a string -- fell into S0720's "name the
// receiver first", advice that cannot help a receiver that IS a name. The
// compiler knows the declared type at that arm (resolve's one hop already
// carried it), so the refusal can say what is true: the type, the word it does
// not have, and per-type advice in {3} -- the variant's row names its four
// words and `held()`, because M12 is where a wrong selector became an easy
// mistake to make. It stays a RUN-time refusal like S0720, for S0720's own
// reason: a wrong question in a branch that never runs is a program that runs.
//
// AND ONE ROW IS CHOSEN BY THE SELECTOR RATHER THAN BY THE TYPE: `as_number`
// on a `satellite.variable.hex`. M19.5 gave that row to binary `1 6 5 4` and
// deliberately not to hex, so anyone reaching for it here has almost certainly
// just read binary's -- and "its methods are the words numbered under that
// path" answers a question they did not ask. The hex row gives the reason
// instead: `as_number` reads the characters as an ordinary decimal and `A` to
// `F` are not decimal digits. The author asked for it by name on 2026-09-09,
// in the same breath as dropping the row.

SAT_CODE(723, EVAL_NO_SUCH_QUESTION, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is not a question a `{2}` answers -- {3}")

// 724 IS THE WRITE-SIDE TWIN OF 721, M15's, and it lives in this decade for
// the decade's own reason: it is about a program being EARLY rather than
// wrong. The retune made assignment to a language path mean something
// (dispatch.hpp's Assigners), four dials accept one today, and a path
// outside those four gets this sentence -- which can LOOSEN row by row the
// day a later milestone installs more write rows, exactly as S0721 answers
// thin out as milestones land handlers.
SAT_CODE(724, EVAL_NOT_RETUNABLE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is not a place a program can write -- what an assignment to "
         "a language path can retune are the dials under "
         "`satellite.library.system`")

// 725 THROUGH 729 ARE M16's, THE CONTAINERS' ROWS, and they are S0715 and
// S0716's shapes retold for a list and a map: refuse by name where another
// language would hand back a sentinel, and beside every refusal name the
// question that answers instead of stopping. 726's second clause is the map's
// own argument, v1's, kept because it is the reason a miss cannot be nil:
// `nothing` is a value an entry can legitimately hold (DESIGN §8.7), so
// "absent" and "present but holding nothing" must never be one answer.

SAT_CODE(725, EVAL_OUTSIDE_THE_LIST, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` was asked about position {2} and this list has {3} elements "
         "-- the first is at 0, the last at one less than the count")

SAT_CODE(726, EVAL_NO_SUCH_KEY, SAT_ERROR,
         "this map holds nothing under {1} -- a missing key is an error "
         "because `nothing` is a value an entry can legitimately hold, and "
         "`has(k)` is the question that answers false instead of stopping")

SAT_CODE(727, EVAL_NOT_A_KEY, SAT_ERROR,
         "a map key is a `satellite.variable.string` or a "
         "`satellite.variable.number`, and this one is {1} -- DESIGN.md "
         "§8.4: a key's hash and equality run inside a mutation, so the key "
         "types are native and closed")

SAT_CODE(728, EVAL_NOT_IN_THE_LIST, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} does not appear in this list, so `{2}` has no position to "
         "answer -- `contains(x)` is the question that answers false instead "
         "of stopping")

SAT_CODE(729, EVAL_EMPTY_LIST, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` was asked of an empty list, which has no element to answer "
         "with -- `empty()` and `size()` are the questions that answer "
         "instead of stopping")

// AN INTERRUPT IS NOBODY'S MISTAKE AND ITS ROW SAYS WHAT IS TRUE INSTEAD OF
// WHAT IS WRONG. Everything above the caret's line ran and was drained through
// the console before this printed; nothing at or after it did. The exit is
// 130 rather than 1 or 4 -- system_facts/interrupt.hpp owns the number and
// machine.hpp's Ending note owns the argument that a Ctrl-C is neither a
// malformed program nor a machine limit.
SAT_CODE(730, EVAL_INTERRUPTED, SAT_ERROR,
         "the run stopped here, at the first statement boundary after Ctrl-C "
         "arrived -- everything before this line ran and nothing at or after "
         "it did")

// 731 IS S0726 FOR THE OBJECT AND NOT S0726 ITSELF, which is one row for one
// reason: S0726's sentence says "this map", and sending somebody who asked
// `arguments.get("machine.thread")` to a map's method table is the mistake
// `satellite.container.arguments` `1 4 3` exists to prevent -- PLAN M20 mints
// that type name for exactly this. The advice is the same and so is the
// argument behind it: a miss cannot answer `nothing`, because two of the
// object's rows are a later milestone's and refuse, so "absent" and "present
// and not answering yet" must never be one answer.
SAT_CODE(731, EVAL_NO_SUCH_ENTRY, SAT_ERROR,
         "the arguments object holds nothing under {1} -- `keys()` lists every "
         "name it answers to and `has(k)` is the question that answers false "
         "instead of stopping")

SAT_CODE(790, NOTE_EVAL_DEEPEST_CALL, SAT_NOTE,
         "the deepest call is here, and the {1} frames under it are not printed")

// --- S08xx -- the machine limits --------------------------------------------
//
// PLAN §4.5's satellite_config.ini, refused rather than ignored. M6's done-when
// asks for "a malformed file refused with a code, a span and a caret", and the
// whole file is refused on the first problem it holds -- unlike a bad `.satc`,
// which S0390 says is ignored while the source is read instead. The difference
// is what the two files ARE: a `.satc` is a cache and deleting it costs nothing,
// while a config is the only statement of how much of this machine satl may
// take, and guessing at that would be DESIGN §1.1's "behind their back" with the
// machine's memory as the stake.
//
// EVERY ONE OF THESE CARRIES A REAL SPAN, which is the difference from S03xx one
// block up. A `.satc` is generated and its span would point a person at a file
// the next sentence tells them to delete; a satellite_config.ini is TYPED, by an
// installer or by hand, and the caret lands under the character that is wrong.
//
// THE WATCHDOG HAS NO CODE HERE AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. When MEMORY_MAX is
// crossed satl prints one line of plain words on stderr and goes, which is what
// M6's done-when asks for. A code is for looking up and a span is for something
// inside a file; a process that has run out of the memory it was allowed has
// neither, and rendering `satl: error S08xx:` over it would dress a shutdown up
// as a diagnostic about a file. programs/opening.cpp already keeps the words for
// everything that is about the RUN rather than about a file, and this is that.

SAT_CODE(801, CONFIG_NOT_A_SETTING, SAT_ERROR,
         "a line in this file is `NAME=VALUE`, a `#` comment or blank -- there "
         "are no [sections] -- and {1} is none of them")

SAT_CODE(802, CONFIG_NO_SUCH_SETTING, SAT_ERROR,
         "this file has no setting called `{1}` -- `satl --limits` lists every "
         "one it has")

SAT_CODE(803, CONFIG_SET_TWICE, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is set twice in this file, and a setting is set once")

SAT_CODE(804, CONFIG_NOT_A_NUMBER, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is a whole number and `{2}` is not one")

SAT_CODE(805, CONFIG_NEEDS_A_UNIT, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is an amount of memory and `{2}` says how much without saying of "
         "what -- write `{2}GiB` for powers of 1024 or `{2}GB` for powers of "
         "1000, because 61.9 GiB and 64.9 GB are the same memory and a bare "
         "number cannot say which was meant")

SAT_CODE(806, CONFIG_NO_SUCH_UNIT, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is not a unit -- B, KiB, MiB, GiB and TiB are powers of 1024, "
         "and KB, MB, GB and TB are powers of 1000")

SAT_CODE(807, CONFIG_TOO_SMALL, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is {2} and the smallest it may be is {3}")

SAT_CODE(808, CONFIG_TOO_LARGE, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is {2} and the largest it may be is {3}")

// SEPARATE FROM S0804 BECAUSE THE FIX IS DIFFERENT AND A SENTENCE HAS TO NAME
// THE FIX. `CORE_COUNT=twelve` is answered by writing a number; `MEMORY_MAX=
// 61.9GiB` is a number, and what is wrong with it is that a ceiling rounded to
// a whole byte behind the user's back is one nobody can predict -- so the fix
// is the next unit down, where the amount they mean is exact. Answering both
// with "is a whole number" would have been one row saying two things, which is
// the failure this file exists to make visible rather than one it may commit.
SAT_CODE(809, CONFIG_NOT_A_WHOLE_AMOUNT, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is a whole number of units and `{2}` is not one -- write the "
         "next unit down, so that the byte count is exact instead of rounded")

// THE THIRD THING A MACHINE SETTING'S VALUE MAY BE WRONG ABOUT, and it is a
// different sentence from S0804 for the reason S0809 is: the sentence has to
// name the fix, and here there are two fixes because there are two things the
// value is allowed to be. `CORE_COUNT=twelve` could have meant a number or it
// could have meant the machine, and a message saying only "is a whole number"
// would send somebody to look up a core count they did not want to write down.
//
// {2} IS THE PATH THIS SETTING TAKES AND IT IS NOT A FIXED STRING. Each of the
// three has exactly one -- DESIGN §7.7's pairing -- so the row is the same
// sentence with a different answer in it, and config_internal.hpp's kSettings
// is where the answer comes from. A file that names the WRONG one of the three
// lands here too, which is right: `CORE_COUNT=arguments.memory.total` is not a
// core count, and the message names the one that is.
//
// This row cannot be raised from example/broken_config.ini -- all three
// settings are spent there on S0805, S0807 and S0808, and naming one twice
// raises S0803 instead -- so tests/limits_test raises it from a string, which
// is that file's own established answer for S0806, S0809 and S0891.
SAT_CODE(810, CONFIG_NOT_A_FACT, SAT_ERROR,
         "{1} is a whole number or `{2}`, which is what this machine says -- "
         "and `{3}` is neither")

SAT_CODE(890, NOTE_CONFIG_SET_HERE, SAT_NOTE,
         "{1} was set here first")

// The parser's S0208 one registry on, and the same argument: a reader stops
// after twenty problems, and without this the twenty-first is invisible and the
// rest of the file looks understood. A config file is a dozen lines and will
// almost never reach it -- which is exactly when a cap goes unnoticed, so it is
// said out loud rather than left to a reader who pointed satl at the wrong file.
SAT_CODE(891, NOTE_CONFIG_TOO_MANY, SAT_NOTE,
         "{1} problems were reported and the rest of this file was not read -- "
         "fix these and run it again")

// --- S09xx -- the clock and the dice -----------------------------------------
//
// M13's block, taken 2026-09-04. 901-910 are `satellite.random`'s, 920-929
// `satellite.time`'s; the gap is the same deliberate one every block keeps.
//
// SIX OF THESE SENTENCES ARE v1's, PORTED AS ROWS. PLAN M13: "what survives is
// the argument checking and v1's nine failure texts, reached through
// handlers[path_id] and reported through M5" -- v1 composed them at its call
// sites (902 through 908 below, near verbatim), and this file is where they
// stop being literals somebody has to find twice. 901, 909 and 910 have no v1
// ancestor because the shapes they refuse were specified 2026-09-04.
//
// AN UNKNOWN TIER IS NOT HERE AND MUST NEVER BE. `satellite.random.quick(5)`
// is M5's did-you-mean over the failing trie level -- S0521/S0523 -- not a
// tenth row in this block; v1 answered it from one generic "no such module
// function" and PLAN §7 throws that dispatch away.

// THE AUTHOR'S TEXT, VERBATIM AFTER THE DASH, one text for all three tiers and
// both spellings (2026-09-04): the zero-argument call `fast()` `1 7 1` and the
// bare path `satellite.random.fast` fold to the same number, a path is not a
// value, and the refusal says what to do instead. The answer not taken --
// inferring the digit count from the assignment's destination -- is recorded
// in DESIGN §11 so it is not re-proposed.
SAT_CODE(901, RANDOM_NEEDS_A_SHAPE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` draws nothing on its own -- you must supply a digit_count, "
         "min and max, or min max and step")

SAT_CODE(902, RANDOM_NOT_A_DIGIT_COUNT, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` wants a whole number of digits, got {2}")

SAT_CODE(903, RANDOM_DIGITS_OUT_OF_RANGE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` draws between 0 and {2} digits, not {3}")

SAT_CODE(904, RANDOM_WANTS_NUMBERS, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` wants {2} `satellite.variable.number` arguments")

// Whole numbers, because the answer is one: there is no uniform draw over the
// reals between 1 and 100, and quietly rounding the bounds would answer a
// question nobody asked. v1's argument, travelling with v1's sentence.
SAT_CODE(905, RANDOM_WANTS_WHOLE_NUMBERS, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` wants whole numbers, got {2}")

SAT_CODE(906, RANDOM_RANGE_EMPTY, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is inclusive at both ends, so {2} to {3} is empty")

SAT_CODE(907, RANDOM_RANGE_TOO_WIDE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` spans more than {2} digits")

SAT_CODE(908, RANDOM_COULD_NOT_DRAW, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` could not draw {2} digits")

SAT_CODE(909, RANDOM_STEP_NOT_A_STEP, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` steps forward by a whole number of one or more, not {2}")

// THE REFUSAL NAMES THE LAST VALUE THE STEP REACHES -- the author's rule,
// 2026-09-04, DESIGN §11: `step` must divide `max - min` exactly, so
// `fast(1, 10, 4)` is refused naming 9, and `fast(1, 9, 4)` is what the
// program meant or the program is wrong. "Inclusive at both ends" stays true
// of every range call that answers; a refusal can loosen later, and a draw
// that silently never says `max` is forever.
SAT_CODE(910, RANDOM_STEP_MISSES, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is inclusive at both ends, and stepping by {2} from {3} lands "
         "last on {4}, never {5} -- refused rather than quietly never "
         "answering {5}")

// THE UNIT IS SECONDS, WHOLE OR FRACTIONAL -- the author, 2026-09-04. One
// unit always, so there is nothing at a call site to misread, and 0.09 is an
// exact Number (DESIGN §8.1), so the fraction costs no float. What this row
// refuses is a LENGTH no wait can be: negative, or wider than the int64
// nanosecond count the timer runs on. A wrong TYPE is S0713's sentence, not
// this one's.
SAT_CODE(920, TIME_NOT_A_LENGTH, SAT_ERROR,
         "`satellite.time.sleep` waits a number of seconds, and {1} is not a "
         "length of time it can wait")

// --- S10xx -- the console's input --------------------------------------------
//
// M14's block, taken 2026-09-04. Three rows, and the interrupted case is
// DELIBERATELY NOT A FOURTH: a Ctrl-C at the prompt answers nothing and the
// walk stops itself at the next statement boundary with S0730's own caret --
// the same shape sleep takes, one mechanism for every blocked wait.
//
// 1002 AND 1003 ARE RAISED AT COMPILE TIME, BEFORE THE PROMPT PRINTS, which
// is PLAN M14's done-when clause 5: a target that cannot receive the line
// must fail before anybody types an answer that is then thrown away.

// v1's sentence, a row now: "satellite.console.input reached the end of
// input". End of input is a REAL condition and not a failure of the asking --
// a program run with its stdin closed reaches it immediately -- and the
// refusal is what stops that program from looping on empty answers forever,
// which is M14's clause 6 and §6's do-not-rediscover regression the other
// way round.
SAT_CODE(1001, CONSOLE_END_OF_INPUT, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` reached the end of input -- there is nobody left to answer, "
         "and a program run with its stdin closed reaches this at once")

SAT_CODE(1002, CONSOLE_TARGET_NOT_A_PLACE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` writes its answer into its second argument, so that argument "
         "has to be a variable -- the only out parameter in the language, and "
         "deliberately not the start of a general facility")

SAT_CODE(1003, CONSOLE_ANSWER_IS_THE_PLACE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` writes a place and yields nothing -- it is a statement of its "
         "own, and what it read is in the variable it wrote")

// --- S11xx -- satellite.help ------------------------------------------------
//
// THE BLOCK IS SMALL BECAUSE HELP REFUSES IN ONLY THREE WAYS, and all three are
// about the ARGUMENT rather than about help. `satellite.help` and
// `satellite.help()` cannot fail: they walk a table that is in the binary.
//
// AND ALL THREE ARE DECIDED BY THE COMPILER, WHICH IS WHAT `1 19 1`'s ARGUMENT
// BEING UNEVALUATED BUYS. words.def's fifth list says the argument is a path or
// a name and is never run, so whether it is one is known before the program
// starts -- there is no arrangement of inputs that turns a good ask into a bad
// one. They are still RUN-time refusals, raised through op_misuse, for the
// reason S0720 gives and M14's place arguments follow: a mistake in a branch
// that never runs is a program that runs.

// 1101 IS THE DONE-WHEN'S SECOND SELF-VERIFYING CHECK, AND IT HAD TO BE A NEW
// CODE. `satellite.help(satellite.network)` refused in plain words before this
// milestone existed -- with S0721, because the ARGUMENT dispatched and died
// before help was entered -- and it refused identically for
// `satellite.help(satellite.console)`, which is built. A check that passes
// through the wrong mechanism and gives the same answer to the central question
// help exists to answer is worse than a check that fails, so the sentence that
// answers it now is its own, says what help is for, and names the one command
// that lists the alternative.
SAT_CODE(1101, HELP_NOT_BUILT, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is a path satellite has a number for and nothing built behind "
         "it yet, so there is nothing to tell you about it -- "
         "`satellite.help()` lists what is built")

// 1102 IS THE BARE WORD, and the author settled its sentence on 2026-09-07
// along with the decision that quotes are gone: a topic is reached by its path,
// `satellite.help(satellite.random)`, and `satellite.help()` is how you find
// the path. So the two things a bare word can be are covered in one sentence --
// a variable this program declared, or a word of the language somebody left the
// `satellite.` off.
SAT_CODE(1102, HELP_NO_SUCH_NAME, SAT_ERROR,
         "there is no variable with the name `{1}` -- did you mean "
         "`satellite.{1}`? `satellite.help()` lists the topics")

// 1103 IS EVERYTHING ELSE, and it exists because the argument's grammar is
// wider than its meaning: `satellite.help("random")` and `satellite.help(1 + 1)`
// both parse. v1 accepted the quoted form for seven topic pages; this language
// does not, and a refusal that says what IS accepted is the difference between
// a rule and a wall.
SAT_CODE(1103, HELP_NOT_A_TOPIC, SAT_ERROR,
         "`satellite.help` takes a path or the name of a variable, and {1} is "
         "neither -- write `satellite.help(satellite.console)` for a topic or "
         "`satellite.help()` for the list")

// 1104 IS A GLOBAL, AND IT IS ITS OWN SENTENCE BECAUSE 1103'S WAS FALSE ABOUT
// IT. `satellite.help(satellite.library.n)` got "`n` is neither a path nor the
// name of a variable" -- and `n` IS the name of a variable, which is the one
// thing a refusal must not get wrong. Found while probing the shapes after the
// milestone was otherwise finished.
//
// AND THE REASON IS A PROPERTY OF THE LANGUAGE RATHER THAN OF HELP. A global is
// written `satellite.library.n = 0` and there is no typed form: DESIGN §6's
// top_level takes `satellite.library.<name>` and S0204 refuses
// `satellite.variable.number satellite.library.n = 0` outright. So resolve has
// no declared type to hand over, and `1 19 1`'s argument is a path or a name
// whose type is known STATICALLY -- help would have to run the program to
// answer, which is the one thing this row is built not to do.
SAT_CODE(1104, HELP_GLOBAL_HAS_NO_TYPE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` is a global, and a global is declared without a type -- help "
         "answers for the type a name was declared with, and a global does not "
         "have one until the program has run")

// --- S12xx -- persistence, files and directories ----------------------------
//
// M19's block, taken 2026-09-08. EIGHT ROWS -- seven written up front and 1208
// added the same day, when a help example ran and 1203 said something false --
// AND THE SMALLNESS IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE MODULE: DESIGN §9 makes a failed OPEN a value and not an error,
// so the commonest thing that goes wrong with a file -- it is not there --
// raises nothing at all. The handle comes back holding `errno`, `ok` `1 6 2 8`
// asks, `error` `1 6 2 10` says why, and the program carries on. That is
// PLAN M19's done-when clause "open a path that does not exist, print the
// reason, and keep running", and it is the one assertion in the milestone that
// cannot be faked.
//
// SO WHAT IS LEFT IS THE SPLIT v1 DREW AND THIS BLOCK KEEPS: a PROGRAM mistake
// is an error and a RUNTIME failure is a value. Asking a read-only handle to
// write is a program that is wrong however the disk behaves; a write that fills
// the disk is a false the caller can test. Every row below is on the first side
// of that line, which is why none of them is about a syscall failing.
//
// 1201 IS WHY THE MODE WORDS DO NOT NEED TO FOLD. WORD_NUMBERS §1.5's literal
// fold would have made a bad mode word M5's *suggester*, over siblings named
// `open_read` and the rest; the author settled on 2026-09-08 that they do not
// fold, so this is M5's *reporter* instead -- a code, a span and a caret, with
// all four words named in the sentence rather than one guessed at. It names the
// four by listing them because there are four, which is a list a reader can act
// on; a "did you mean" over four candidates is a guess where an enumeration is
// available.
//
// AND THERE IS NO "text" OR "binary" MODE, which v1 refused at length and
// DESIGN §8.5 now records: a `satellite.variable.string` already holds
// arbitrary bytes and POSIX has no newline translation to switch off, so the
// word would arrange nothing while telling a program something had been
// arranged. A reader who types one lands here and the sentence is the same
// sentence, deliberately -- there is no special case saying "no such mode, and
// here is a paragraph", because the four words ARE the answer.
SAT_CODE(1201, FILE_BAD_MODE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` opens a file in one of four modes -- \"read\", \"write\", "
         "\"append\" or \"read_append\" -- and {2} is not one of them")

// 1202 IS THE MISTAKE FOUR MODES MADE ORDINARY. While every mode went one way,
// asking a handle for the other direction produced EBADF, and "Bad file
// descriptor" is the wrong sentence about a descriptor in perfect health --
// v1's note, and its `readable`/`writable` fields existed for a milestone
// before anything read them. The direction is checked BEFORE the syscall and
// the message names the modes that would have worked, because the fix is at the
// open and not at the call that noticed.
SAT_CODE(1202, FILE_WRONG_DIRECTION, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` cannot {2} `{3}`: this `satellite.variable.file` was not opened "
         "for it -- open it with {4}")

// 1203 IS A CLOSED HANDLE, AND IT IS AN ERROR RATHER THAN A FALSE BECAUSE
// DESIGN §8's "never silently reopen" leaves nothing else it could be. A
// program that reads after closing has a mistake in it that no disk state can
// excuse, and answering false would make it indistinguishable from an empty
// file. `open` `1 6 2 2` is the spelling that reopens, and the sentence names
// it so the reader is not left to guess that closing is reversible.
SAT_CODE(1203, FILE_IS_CLOSED, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` was asked of a `satellite.variable.file` that is closed -- "
         "`open` reopens one, and a handle whose open failed answers `ok` false "
         "rather than raising")

// 1204 IS THE ONE FAILURE IN `satellite.directory` THAT IS AN ERROR AND NOT A
// VALUE, AND M19 RE-AFFIRMED IT RATHER THAN INHERITING IT. `.list` has nowhere
// to put bad news: the empty list is already a true answer about an empty
// directory, so spending it on "there was no directory" makes the two
// indistinguishable -- the silent wrong answer DESIGN §9 exists to refuse, and
// the one that survives every other fix. Loud costs a caller one `exists(d)`;
// quiet costs them a program that lists a mistyped path as empty and does
// nothing. The sentence names `exists(d)` for exactly that reason.
SAT_CODE(1204, DIRECTORY_NO_SUCH, SAT_ERROR,
         "`satellite.directory.list` has no such directory {1} -- "
         "`satellite.directory.exists` asks that without failing")

SAT_CODE(1205, DIRECTORY_NOT_A_DIRECTORY, SAT_ERROR,
         "`satellite.directory.list` wants a directory, and {1} is a file")

// 1206 IS A DIRECTORY THAT IS THERE AND CANNOT BE READ, WHICH IS A THIRD THING
// AND NOT A SHADE OF 1204. `exists` answers TRUE for it -- stat needs only
// search permission on the parent -- so `exists` is a safe way to ask about
// ENOENT and is not a promise that `list` will succeed, and a reader who has
// just been sent to `exists` by 1204 has to be able to find that out here.
//
// IT ALSO COVERS A LISTING THAT STOPPED PART WAY, v1's case: a half-read
// directory is worse than none, because its `.size()` is a number the caller
// would believe.
SAT_CODE(1206, DIRECTORY_UNREADABLE, SAT_ERROR,
         "`satellite.directory.list` cannot read {1}: {2}")

// 1207 IS `satellite.system.delete` REFUSING TO STRINGIFY. v1's note is the
// argument and it is a sharp one: `to_string(7)` is "7", which is a filename
// the filesystem would accept, so a number that reached this call by mistake
// would delete a file named after itself and answer true. A destructive verb
// gets the strictest argument check in the language, and nil gets its own half
// of the sentence because the way a program arrives at nil here is almost
// always a `satellite.variable.file` that was declared and never opened.
SAT_CODE(1207, DELETE_NOT_A_NAME, SAT_ERROR,
         "`satellite.system.delete` names what to remove with a "
         "`satellite.variable.string` path or an open `satellite.variable.file`, "
         "and {1} is neither -- a declared file holds nothing until "
         "`satellite.file.open` or `satellite.file.new` gives it a path")

// 1208 IS THE HANDLE THAT NEVER OPENED, AND IT EXISTS BECAUSE 1203'S SENTENCE
// WAS FALSE ABOUT IT -- the same shape S1104 has beside S1103 one block up, and
// found the same way: by running the thing and reading what it said.
//
// A leftover file from an earlier run made `satellite.file.new` come back
// EEXIST, and the next line of the program said "`write_line` was asked of a
// `satellite.variable.file` that is CLOSED" -- about a handle that had never
// been open, three lines under the call that failed to open it, while the errno
// explaining the whole thing sat unread in `error`. A message that points away
// from the answer is worse than a shorter one.
//
// SO IT NAMES THE PATH AND CARRIES THE REASON, because the fix is at the
// constructor and not at the line that noticed. The two states are told apart
// by `FileHandle::ever_open` and not by whether an errno is held -- a handle
// that opened, failed one write and was then closed holds an errno and HAS
// been open, so the obvious derivation is the wrong one.
SAT_CODE(1208, FILE_NEVER_OPENED, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` was asked of a `satellite.variable.file` that never opened -- "
         "`{2}`: {3}. `ok` answers that without stopping, and `error` is this "
         "same reason")

// --- S13xx -- the machine's facts -------------------------------------------
//
// M20. `satellite.system` answers what the machine is, and the one way a
// program can get that wrong is to name a unit that does not exist.
//
// ONE CODE FOR THE WHOLE NAMESPACE, and that is the shape rather than a start.
// Every other refusal these paths can make is already somebody's: a unit given
// as a number instead of a string is S0711's wrong type, and a path that is
// numbered and not built is S0721. What is left is a string that IS a string
// and is not one of the five words -- which is exactly the shape S1201 has for
// a file's four modes, one block down, and the sentence is written to match it
// because the mistake is the same mistake.

// THE FIVE WORDS ARE THE ANSWER, so the message spells all five rather than
// saying "not a unit" and leaving the reader to find the list. That is S1201's
// choice and its reason: "the four words ARE the answer".
//
// AND IT NAMES THE DEFAULT, because a program that meant megabytes can simply
// stop passing anything -- which is the fix more often than picking a different
// word is.
SAT_CODE(1301, SYSTEM_BAD_UNIT, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` reports memory in one of five units -- \"b\", \"kb\", \"mb\", "
         "\"gb\" or \"tb\" -- and {2} is not one of them. Asked with no unit "
         "it answers in \"mb\"")

// 1302 IS AN ENUMERATION AND NOT A DID-YOU-MEAN, which is M19's decision at
// S1201 over the four file modes, reached again here over a list of ONE. A
// suggester guesses where an enumeration is available, and "disabled" is the
// whole of what this dial accepts besides a number.
//
// AND THE NUMBER IS NAMED IN THE SENTENCE, because the mistake it catches is
// almost always somebody reaching for the off switch: `min_free_mb = 0` is a
// floor of zero and stays armed, which is the author's decision of
// 2026-09-11, and the sentence says so where it will be read.
SAT_CODE(1302, SYSTEM_BAD_DIAL_WORD, SAT_ERROR,
         "`{1}` takes a whole number of megabytes or the word \"disabled\", "
         "and {2} is neither -- `0` is a floor of zero and keeps the watch "
         "armed, so \"disabled\" is how a program stops it")

#undef SAT_CODE
